living paycheck to paycheck so as we're  trying to become more happy through  purchases or by other external means we  might be actually digging ourselves  deeper and deeper into an unhappiness  trap if we look for our happiness in  external things like money power over  others what other people think about us  and so on there will always be something  else to desire the truth is if you can't  be happy now you probably won't be happy  in  future because a you may die before you  achieve your goals B you might realize  how empty you feel after accomplishing  them and as a result might quickly move  on to the next goal and see by constant  looking for happiness in the future and  design what you don't have you're  actually wiring your brain to always  look for happiness elsewhere in this way  people become slaves to their desires  forever chasing happiness without ever  really experiencing it the Tower of God  is pretty much our desires on steroids  it presents itself as being able to  fulfill anybody's desire no matter who's  and no matter how big in other words the  tower is supposed to be something that  can grant anyone happiness in reality  though people come to the tower to get  something but in most cases they end up  losing everything they had things that  they might have taken for granted until  it's too late  thus it's very similar to how us  desiring to keep up with the Joneses in  the modern world can lead us down a  death spiral where we end up in a much  worse position than we were initially in  part of what good art like Tower of God  does in my view is it helps us see our  own faults with more clarity so that we  can live a better life in the real world  one of the things terribly emphasizes is  taking the things we have for granted  while seeking what we don't have or in  other words assuming the grass is always  greener on the other side the story  isn't just talking to us the readers in  the beginning as I ate it it's  communicating with us throughout even  the fact that Rachel wanted to move up  the tower so that she could see the real  endless sky with its countless stars  begs us to examine ourselves and how we  take such things for granted if you're  anything like me you probably take the  beautiful night sky for granted most  nights because it's always there  you can go days weeks or even months  without appreciating it in contrast we  may be mesmerized by a huge mansion or  expensive car because it's not something  we can afford on autopilot we tend to  undervalue what we have an over value  what we don't but the author of power of  God is asking us to re-evaluate things  for a second look at Rachel look how  driven she is to see the real open sky  the sense of freedom that comes with  such a view especially if you've never  seen it before for her and everyone else  in that  world the sky would be more impressive  and they pay more to see it than any car  in the same way we shouldn't assume that  taking a date to a super expensive  restaurant that we can't afford is  better than going to a picnic nearby for  a fraction of the price as the  philosopher Epictetus said wealth  consists not in having great possessions  but in having few wants and quote in  other words if you want true wealth and  happiness it's better to count your  blessings rather than always dwell on  what's missing that doesn't mean you  shouldn't pursue any goals but you  should pursue them happily while  appreciating what you have along the way  and you should be okay with the  possibility of not achieving a specific  goal your happiness should not be tied  to external outcomes that are outside of  your complete control give it your best  shot if it's something that means a lot  to you but realize that the destination  for everyone is death so there's no  excuse for not enjoying the journey I  think there are problems with the  protagonist Baum's philosophy too but  what sets him apart from most other  people in the story is that he didn't  want to go to the tower Rachel wanted  more but Baum was good he spent his life  in a dark cave and he was happy because  Rachel came to visit him every so often  Baum didn't need a harem or money or  power he was happy with the way things  were even though most people watching  this video probably can't imagine being  happy if they were stuck in a dark cave  for as long as they could remember it  wasn't enough for Rachel and she even  got to leave the cave she desired more  and so did the countless people that  continued to try to climb up the tower  Baum isn't a conventional Shonen  character whose main goal is to become  the best or the strongest in the world I  pointed this out about Demon Slayer 2  where Tanja rose desire was to save  Natsuko not to be the number one Demon  Slayer in the world Baum similarly was  happy with what he had even if it wasn't  much and prioritized human connection  rather than money power fame and so on  unfortunately for him rachel is known as  Zuko and she clearly doesn't want to be  saved  here lies Baum's problem simple is not  the answer just like money and fame are  external things that can easily bring  you misery if you completely tie your  happiness to them Baum will stay unhappy  if he continues to tie as happiness  a girl that wants nothing to do with him  anymore here's another relevant quote  from Epictetus it's not what happens to  you but how you react to it that matters  end quote we should empathize with bomb  after all it's hard when you love  someone and just want to be with them  and they want something else however he  can't control Rachael's feelings and  should ultimately let go  instead of being unhappy and  jeopardizing his life and other lives  while chasing Rachel he should  appreciate the new friends around him  like coolin and rock and the girls who  actually like him and are willing to  chase after him too through watching and  reading Tower of God I'm constantly  reminded to actually look her out 